Static hosts: a root `404.html` is served for every path with no file (Netlify and Cloudflare Pages docs). Never add a catch-all `/* /index.html 200` rewrite to `_redirects` or vercel.json: it turns every unknown URL into a 200 (RTE-004). On Cloudflare Pages a root 404.html must exist, otherwise Pages assumes a single-page app and serves the root for unknown paths. Check the status after deploying: `curl -sI https://example.com/sg-probe` must say 404 (the docs do not spell out the status line; UNPROVEN until you run it). Neither Netlify `_redirects` nor Cloudflare Pages `_redirects` documents status 410 (Cloudflare lists only 301/302/303/307/308 and 200), so 410 comes from a tiny edge function listing the retired paths (use the Netlify file or the Cloudflare pair; `_routes.json` goes in the publish dir and keeps static requests from invoking the function). A static host cannot produce a 5xx for your pages; a 5xx (500/502/503) belongs to the API or serverless function behind a form or widget and must be returned there on failure (RTE-007), and an outage of the host itself is the host's.
